{
  "dom_id": "step-37798",
  "name": "Edge Cases",
  "problem": "Okay, so we've seen that a geometric sequence is convergent for $|r| < 1$ and divergent when $|r| >1.$ But what about when $|r| = 1?$\n\nWe have two cases:\n\n- When $r=1,$ the geometric sequence is convergent and converges to the first term. \n\nFor example, consider the sequence $c_n = 2(1)^n,$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14143612/assets/6f11903fd259e75a51de9877fadbb97e975b48bce229bad954aae2d6b3da36c9.png)\n\nIn this case, the sequence is convergent, and the limit of the sequence is $c_1 = 2.$\n\n- When $r=-1,$ the geometric sequence is divergent. \n\nFor example, consider the sequence $d_n = 2(-1)^n,$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14143612/assets/3e4de425d4e6af816380b275e536685f524adfb33643715b093d6def3762f03a.png)\n\nIn this case, the sequence oscillates between $2$ and $-2.$ It does not approach a fixed number, so it is divergent.",
